        {
          "name": "Learn more",
          "text": "CMatrixLab\nby Martin Grossman\nmartin.grossman@gmail.com\nGeneric ML\nSimple view\nReset ↺\n\nYour flu test is positive. What’s the chance you really have flu? That’s precision—and it also depends on how common flu is among the people tested.\nCompare 10% and 50% sick using the same test. Precision changes, even though the portion of sick people correctly testing positive (TPR) and the portion of people without flu getting a false alarm (FPR) stay the same.\n\nTPR\nFixed with sick portion\n96%\nTP ÷ (TP + FN)\nFPR\nFixed with sick portion\n4%\nFP ÷ (FP + TN)\nPrecision\nDepends on sick portion\n96%\nTP ÷ (TP + FP)\nSensitivity to the flu signal\n50%\n0% · Less sensitive\n100% · More sensitive\nHow different the two groups are\n60%\nHard to tell apart\nEasy to tell apart\nSick portion\n10%\n50%\n50%\n0% · no one has flu\n100% · everyone has flu\nTP\n· Flu detected\nFP\n· False alarm\nFN\n· Flu missed\nTN\n· No flu, test −\n\nFull marker = 10 people; smaller = partial.\n\nTest −\nTest +\nActual flu\n500 people\nActual no flu\n500 people\n0.00\n0.25\n0.50\n0.75\n1.00\nFlu test score →\nTest −\nTest +\nActual flu\n\n500 people\nFN · Flu missed\n20\nTP · Flu detected\n480\nActual no flu\n\n500 people\nTN · No flu, test −\n480\nFP · False alarm\n20\nAmong positive tests: 480 TP ÷ (480 TP + 20 FP) = 96% Precision.\nGreen: 480 have flu\nRed: 20 false alarms\n\nA CLOSER LOOK\n\nYou tested positive. What is the chance you really have the flu?\nSimple view\nThe basics\nFormulas\nBayes\nA test’s accuracy on sick people is not your chance of being sick.\n\nWhen you get a Test +, you don’t know whether you came from the sick group or the healthy group. Right now, out of 1,000 people, 500 have the flu (yielding 480 true catches) and 500 are healthy (yielding 20 false alarms). Your actual chance of having the flu is 480 out of all 500 positive tests (96%).\n\nSee the step-by-step Bayes proof →\n50%\nBefore · sick portion (p)\n→\n96%\nAfter Test + · Precision\nLikelihood Ratio (LR+ = TPR ÷ FPR): 24× · How strongly a Test + boosts the odds of flu\nTPR\n96%\n\nIf a person really has the flu, what is the chance the test catches it?\n\nP(test + | flu) · Row rate · Fixed when sick portion changes\n\nFPR\n4%\n\nIf a person does NOT have the flu, what is the chance of a false alarm?\n\nP(test + | no flu) · Row rate · Fixed when sick portion changes\n\nPrecision\n96%\n\nIf a person gets a positive test (Test +), what is the chance they truly have the flu?\n\nP(flu | test +) · Column rate · Changes with sick portion\n\nFour possible outcomes (2×2 Confusion Matrix)\n1,000 weighted people · arranged in the exact same 2×2 positions as the matrix above\nTop-left · Sick, Test −\nFN · 20\nFlu missed: person really has the flu, but the test came back negative (Test −).\nTop-right · Sick, Test +\nTP · 480\nFlu caught: person really has the flu, and the test came back positive (Test +).\nBottom-left · Healthy, Test −\nTN · 480\nCorrectly cleared: person does not have the flu, and the test came back negative (Test −).\nBottom-right · Healthy, Test +\nFP · 20\nFalse alarm: person does not have the flu, yet the test came back positive (Test +).\n\nWhy a rare disease creates mostly false alarms. When flu is rare (say 10% sick), 900 out of 1,000 people are healthy. Even a small 4% false-alarm rate on 900 healthy people creates 36 false alarms—diluting the 96 true catches down to 72.7% Precision.\n\nWith the signal sensitivity and score separation fixed, changing the sick portion changes Precision while TPR and FPR stay fixed wherever both rates are defined.\n\nHow this demonstration works\n\n1,000 weighted people. Counts are expected values and may be fractional. Sick portion changes counts and precision; TPR and FPR stay fixed at the same signal sensitivity and score separation."
        }
