        "text": "CMatrixLab\nby Martin Grossman\nmartin.grossman@gmail.com\nGeneric ML\nReset ↺\n\nYour flu test is positive. What’s the chance you really have flu? That’s precision—and it also depends on how common flu is among the people tested.\nCompare 10% and 50% sick using the same test. Precision changes, even though the share of sick people correctly testing positive (TPR) and the share of people without flu getting a false alarm (FPR) stay the same.\n\nTPR\nFixed with sick share\n96%\nFPR\nFixed with sick share\n4%\nPrecision\nDepends on sick share\n96%\nSensitivity to the flu signal\n50%\n0% · Less sensitive\n100% · More sensitive\nHow different the two groups are\n60%\nHard to tell apart\nEasy to tell apart\nSick share\n10%\n50%\n50%\n0% · no one has flu\n100% · everyone has flu\nTP\n· Flu detected\nFP\n· False alarm\nFN\n· Flu missed\nTN\n· No flu, test −\n\nFull dot or square = 10 people; smaller = partial.\n\nTest −\nTest +\nActual flu\n500 people\nActual no flu\n500 people\n0.00\n0.25\n0.50\n0.75\n1.00\nFlu test score →\nTest −\nTest +\nActual flu\n\n500 people\nFN\n20\nTP\n480\nActual no flu\n\n500 people\nTN\n480\nFP\n20\nAmong positive tests: 480 TP ÷ (480 TP + 20 FP) = 96% Precision.\nGreen: 480 have flu\nRed: 20 false alarms\n\n1,000 weighted people. Counts are expected values and may be fractional. Sick share changes counts and precision; TPR and FPR stay fixed at the same signal sensitivity and score separation.",
